---
title = "Render HTML with Lustre"
meta_title = "How to render HTML with Lustre"
subtitle = "Good for server side rendering and static sites"
description = "The Lustre package provides HTML building functionality that works with Gleam on both Erlang and JavaScript"
tags = ["html"]
---

The Lustre package can be used as a type-safe HTML builder library. Add it as a
dependency to your package:

```cli
gleam add lustre
```

Use the functions from Lustre's [`html`](https://lustre.hexdocs.pm/lustre/element/html.html)
and [`html`](https://lustre.hexdocs.pm/lustre/attribute.html) modules to build
some HTML.

The [`element.to_string`](https://lustre.hexdocs.pm/lustre/element.html#to_string)
function can then be used to render the HTML to a string. If the HTML
is a full page and should have a doctype then use the
[`element.to_document_string`](https://lustre.hexdocs.pm/lustre/element.html#to_document_string)
function instead.

```gleam
import lustre/element
import lustre/element/html

pub fn html_page() -> String {
  let html = html.h1([], [
    html.text("Hello, Joe!"),
  ])
  element.to_document_string(html)
}
```
